          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "**What the thyroid does:** The thyroid is a gland in the front of the neck. It mainly produces thyroxine, which the body converts into the more active triiodothyronine. Together these hormones set the metabolic rate of most tissues, meaning how fast the body uses energy (Hypothyroidism, General clinical reference).\n\n**What goes wrong in hypothyroidism:** Hypothyroidism means the thyroid does not make enough thyroid hormone. The pituitary gland, in the brain, senses the low level and releases more thyroid-stimulating hormone to push the thyroid harder. So a failing thyroid usually shows a rising stimulating hormone before the thyroid hormone itself drops out of range. This is why that stimulating hormone is the first blood test. If it is raised, the thyroid hormone level is measured next (Hypothyroidism: testing and monitoring guidance). The reference material does not fully explain the causes in the passages I retrieved, though one guidance note says that antibody tests can show autoimmune thyroiditis (the immune system attacking the thyroid) as the cause.\n\n**How it is classified:**\n- **Primary hypothyroidism:** the thyroid gland itself is at fault. This is the great majority of cases.\n- **Central hypothyroidism:** the pituitary or hypothalamus (a brain region above it) is at fault. This is uncommon, and its blood test pattern differs from the usual one.\n- **Overt versus subclinical:** overt means the stimulating hormone is raised and thyroid hormone is low. Subclinical means the stimulating hormone is raised but thyroid hormone is still within the normal range.\n- **ICD-10-CM coding:** the coding chapter places thyroid disorders, including hypothyroidism, in the E00 to E07 block. I could not retrieve the exact code, because my search for the classification detail failed.\n\nIf you have symptoms or test results, a clinician can interpret them for you. This is general information, not a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
